{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-343359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}-1, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 3, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\pi .$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\pi .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\pi .$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(\\pi ) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=3 \\\\ {f}^{+}(\\pi ) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(\\pi )+{f}^{+}(\\pi )}{2}=\\frac{3+(-1)}{2}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9c27f888db73e9218f13f2a0fd95417ea6166039b0f201dbe1e72e822dea2850"} {"question" "q-343359" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343359" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4, & x\\in [-5,0] \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & x\\in (0,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $10$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=-3.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $10$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=-3.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf(-3)=(-3)^{2}-4=5.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9c27f888db73e9218f13f2a0fd95417ea6166039b0f201dbe1e72e822dea2850"} {"question" "q-343390" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343390" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{3},0] \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & x\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{-}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=2sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9c27f888db73e9218f13f2a0fd95417ea6166039b0f201dbe1e72e822dea2850"} {"question" "q-343385" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343385" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343372" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}-2x+1, & x\\in [-4,0] \\\\ {x}^{2}+2, & x\\in (0,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=-3.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343372" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=-3.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf(-3)=-2(-3)+1=7.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343372" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9c27f888db73e9218f13f2a0fd95417ea6166039b0f201dbe1e72e822dea2850"} {"question" "q-343372" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343372" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343370" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343370" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+1=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343370" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9c27f888db73e9218f13f2a0fd95417ea6166039b0f201dbe1e72e822dea2850"} {"question" "q-343370" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343370" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}-1, & x\\in [-2,0] \\\\ 2, & x\\in (0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{1}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.At $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=1,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(1)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{2})=sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi )=(-1)^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=1,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(1)=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi \\cdot 1}{2}) \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n+1} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9c27f888db73e9218f13f2a0fd95417ea6166039b0f201dbe1e72e822dea2850"} {"question" "q-343429" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343429" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-1)^{n+1}"] ["math" "(-1)^{n}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343429" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-1)^{n+1}"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} {"question" "q-343429" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070509/state.json" "file_sha256" "8911c50d090842de3ae01755710c0fb34b50ac855d8d5af09e7a6a44a50dd853"} 